Title: resolver_shape()'s query_messages_sent is unscoped, misses sendmsg(), ignores return code
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-03T19:59Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

`bench/probe_getaddrinfo_shape.py`'s `resolver_shape()` (added in PR #326,
ticket 0629) derives, from a raw strace trace and the sandbox's already-
classified `Attempt` list, two committed-artifact facts: `connect_outcomes`
(errno bucket per DNS-detector connect) and `query_messages_sent` (count of
resolver query payloads). Multiple independent review passes on PR #326 found
that this function's general correctness does not match the specific
correctness of the three numbers it currently ships — the committed artifact
(`bench/results/0629-gap-a/syscall-shape.json`) is right, but only because
this machine's trace happens to avoid every edge case below. This ticket is
the hardening pass, not a claim that the shipped numbers are wrong.

The artifact is explicitly drift-blind by design (the test docstring: "nothing
re-invokes the probe... a machine that has drifted underneath an unchanged
record is invisible to this file by construction") and `resolver_shape` has
exactly one call site. That is why this is a follow-up rather than a PR #326
blocker — none of these gaps fire on the committed data, and the function
is not (yet) reused elsewhere.

## Findings, by reviewer confidence

1. **`query_messages_sent` is not scoped to resolver traffic.** The outer
   filter (`"sendmmsg(" in line or "sendto(" in line`) scans the *entire*
   trace, not just lines tied to an `attempt.detector == "dns"` connect —
   unlike `connect_outcomes`, which does filter by detector. Any non-DNS UDP
   send elsewhere in a real trace (QUIC, any other protocol) would be folded
   into the same counter.
2. **Plain `sendmsg(` is invisible.** `sandbox.py`'s own `_SYSCALLS` traces
   `("connect", "sendto", "sendmsg")` as equally significant, but the filter
   only matches `"sendmmsg("` or `"sendto("` — `"sendmsg("` is a substring of
   neither. A resolver that queries via unbatched `sendmsg` instead of
   `sendmmsg` would report 0 messages sent for an arm that did send.
3. **No return-code check before counting a message as sent.** A failed
   `sendto`/`sendmmsg` (`= -1 E...`), or a `sendmmsg` partial send (return <
   array length), is still counted at its full attempted length —
   `query_messages_sent` measures messages *attempted*, not *sent*, despite
   its name and the README's framing.
4. **`_OUTCOME`'s bare `.search()` can grab the wrong `=<digits>` on IPv6.**
   `_OUTCOME = re.compile(r"=\s*-?\d+\s*(?P<errno>E[A-Z]+)?")` returns the
   leftmost match. An IPv6 connect line carries `sin6_scope_id=<N>` before the
   trailing return code; when the scope id is a bare digit (commonly `0`),
   `.search()` binds there instead of the real `= -1 E...` at the end,
   misclassifying a failed connect as `"ok"`. Reproduced directly against a
   real IPv6 strace line by one reviewer (high confidence); a second reviewer
   found the same class independently (medium confidence, code-reading only).
   IPv4-only on this machine today, so inert against the committed artifact.

## Relevant files

- `bench/probe_getaddrinfo_shape.py` — `resolver_shape()` (~line 83),
  `_OUTCOME`/`_MESSAGE` regexes (~lines 75-80), the `measure()` call site.
- `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py` — `Attempt`, `attempts_in()`, `_SYSCALLS`
  (the syscalls actually traced; `sendmsg` is already one of them).
- `tests/test_probe_getaddrinfo_shape.py` — carries two direct
  `resolver_shape()` unit tests added alongside this ticket (ok/errno path,
  the `unparsed` fallback for a split connect line); neither exercises IPv6
  scope_id, a failed/partial send, or a bare `sendmsg(` line.

## Actions

1. Scope `query_messages_sent` to lines associated with an
   `attempt.detector == "dns"` connect, mirroring `connect_outcomes` — this
   likely needs the trace's send lines correlated to a resolver socket/fd
   rather than scanned globally.
2. Add `sendmsg(` (not just `sendmmsg(`) to the outer filter.
3. Read each send line's own trailing return code; do not count a failed or
   partial send as fully sent.
4. Anchor `_OUTCOME` on the line's trailing return code specifically
   (e.g. `=\s*(-?\d+)\s*(?P<errno>E[A-Z]+)?\s*(?:\(.*\))?\s*$`) rather than a
   bare `.search()`, so an earlier `=<digit>` field (IPv6 `sin6_scope_id`,
   or any future field shaped the same way) cannot be mistaken for it.

## Test

Red-first, per finding: a synthetic trace line with a non-DNS `sendto` mixed
into an otherwise all-DNS trace (finding 1); a synthetic `sendmsg(` line
carrying one query (finding 2); a `sendmmsg` line whose return code is less
than its message-array length (finding 3); a real IPv6 failed-connect line
with `sin6_scope_id=0` (finding 4, reproduced by a reviewer against real
strace output — worth capturing verbatim as a fixture).

## Verification

- [ ] Each of the four findings above has a red-before-green unit test.
- [ ] The three numbers currently committed in
      `bench/results/0629-gap-a/syscall-shape.json` are unchanged by the fix
      (this machine's trace shape doesn't hit any of these paths).
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- `resolver_shape()` stays a pure function of `(trace, attempts)` — no new
  I/O, no re-invocation of the probe.
- The privacy invariant holds: no raw trace text or `attempt.line` enters the
  returned dict or the committed artifact (verified by three separate
  reviewers on PR #326; do not regress it while fixing the above).

## Exit criteria

`resolver_shape()`'s outcome and message-count logic is correct in general —
not just for the three toy programs and the one machine that produced the
currently-committed artifact — with unit coverage for each finding above.
